### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/lv.svg" width="32" alt="Latvia" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />Latvia

Latvia maintains maximum Western institutional integration while openly acknowledging tensions with US policy, particularly regarding Trump administration's Greenland statements and broader transatlantic relationship uncertainties. The country continues its defense spending trajectory above 4.9% of GDP with expanding NATO mission participation beyond immediate geography through Arctic Sentry involvement, while executing scheduled procurement programs including Hunter IFV deliveries and anti-tank mine production facilities. Domestic governance demonstrates continued anti-corruption enforcement through investigation of Agriculture Minister over wood processing contracts, maintaining oversight within coalition dynamics. The sixth unsuccessful no-confidence motion against PM Siliņa confirms the established pattern of opposition messaging without genuine regime threat, as coalition arithmetic prevents alternative majority formation despite persistent political volatility. Latvia's strategic posture remains unchanged: zero hedging toward alternative powers, maximum alliance integration, and vocal Ukraine support as core commitments regardless of bilateral relationship tensions with individual allies.

**Key developments:**

- **Diplomatic:** PM Siliņa told POLITICO that Europe must speak 'direct and clear' to the US in resetting transatlantic ties, stating 'I don't think we will be doing business as usual' following Trump's Greenland threats. President Rinkēvičs emphasized need for increased political and economic pressure on Russia, including enhanced sanctions and fighting Russia's shadow fleet.
- **Diplomatic:** Parliament condemned IOC's decision to disqualify Ukrainian skeleton athlete Vladislav Heraskevichus for honoring fallen athletes on his helmet. Multiple Latvian officials including PM Siliņa and President Rinkēvičs criticized the decision, with Rinkēvičs stating athletes cannot be silenced for showing 'what modern Russia is.'
- **Security:** Defense Minister Sprūds announced Latvia's participation in NATO's new Arctic Sentry mission following Brussels defense ministers meeting. The mission builds on NATO's Arctic security focus, particularly regarding Trump's Greenland annexation statements. Latvia is clarifying type and extent of involvement with allies.
- **Security:** Defense Minister Sprūds announced receipt of first Hunter infantry fighting vehicles and signing of agreements for anti-tank mine production facility during NATO meetings. These represent implementation of existing procurement programs within Latvia's defense industrial development strategy.
- **Economic:** PM Siliņa indicated she may seek Agriculture Minister Krauze's dismissal following service inspection findings on controversial contracts reducing LVM timber prices for private processors. Investigation found two agreements were added to support recipients list without justification, with unclear documentation preparation. Prosecutor and State Audit Office reviews ongoing.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Latvian government officials attend Winter Olympics in Milan-Cortina** — PM Siliņa and President Rinkēvičs attended Winter Olympics events, visiting Latvian athletes and making public appearances. Siliņa was seen trying Olympic ice and attending hockey training sessions. *([sportazinas.com](https://sportazinas.com/gaidam-puisus-majas-ar-medalam-silina-apmekle-hokeja-izlases-treninu-milana))*
- **Former SAB employee sentenced, Latvian citizen convicted of espionage for Russia** — Former Constitution Protection Bureau (SAB) employee Aigars Sparāns had his three-year prison sentence upheld for illegal gathering of state secrets. Separately, a Latvian citizen was sentenced to six years for espionage for Russia. *([tvnet.lv](https://tvnet.lv/8414914/bijusajam-sab-darbiniekam-sparanam-saglaba-cietumsodu))*
- **Eurovision 2026: Atvara to represent Latvia with song 'Ēnā'** — Singer Atvara won the 'Supernova' competition final on February 14 and will represent Latvia at the 70th Eurovision Song Contest with the song 'Ēnā' (In the Shadow). *([lsm.lv](https://lsm.lv/raksts/kultura/izklaide/14.02.2026-latviju-70-eirovizijas-dziesmu-konkursa-parstaves-atvara.a633250))*
